#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088668} :activity/id #uuid "c5111638-8050-5ca1-a687-ed434d6556aa" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049182 :topic/id #uuid "e1819049-d5ad-572e-bf0c-2abb071dc9a4" :topic/math-academy-id 885} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088668 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088851 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to calculate the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x) = e^{3x}.$ Computing this using the formula is a lot of work because we have to calculate many derivatives and then plug them into the formula. Is there any easier way?\n\nYes, there is! First, let's recall the Maclaurin expansion of $e^x{:}$\n\n$$\ne^x = 1 + x + \\dfrac{x^2}{2!} + \\dfrac{x^3}{3!} + \\cdots, \\qquad x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)\n$$\n\nSimply replacing $x$ with $3x$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3x} & =1+(3x)+\\frac{(3x)^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{(3x)^{3}}{3!}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+3x+\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{27{x}^{3}}{6}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+3x+\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{9{x}^{3}}{2}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the new interval of convergence, we do the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & \\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ) \\\\ -\\infty  & <3x<\\infty  \\\\ -\\infty  & <x<\\infty  \\\\ x & \\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the interval of convergence stays the same, and we have\n\n$$\ne^{3x} = 1 + 3x + \\dfrac{9x^2}{2} + \\dfrac{9x^3}{2}  + \\cdots, \\qquad x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! Notice how much quicker this was compared with calculating the expansion from scratch." :tutorial/id #uuid "96030fee-2ce6-5a54-86a7-0e9c64d26bc7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1307 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f4da26a5-2471-58ea-91c9-3d394365dd82" :step/math-academy-id 2570 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088693}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088669 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087787 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087633 :question/id #uuid "895adb0f-347a-52e1-a160-3fbba31a4c49" :question/math-academy-id "e-754"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdd566f7-15e2-59d9-bf6a-1fa3598399b9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050812}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548058}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maclaurin Expansion of a Composite Function Using a Standard Maclaurin Expansion"} :step/id #uuid "bf07391e-3460-58b6-90e7-6c1b023dcdc0" :step/math-academy-id 2571 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088694}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088670 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087788 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087634 :question/id #uuid "7f8c3610-ab9a-5bee-bcc5-a817a79b50cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-755"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffddeaa7-a247-54b0-af4b-a29d9bbeee6a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530469} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530519}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maclaurin Expansion of a Sum of Functions Using Standard Maclaurin Expansions"} :step/id #uuid "668d6691-289b-5210-b9fc-1aafe63d434c" :step/math-academy-id 2574 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088671}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088671 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087789 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087635 :question/id #uuid "e29f6eb6-9b2a-5f03-aad3-3bc36dbe400e" :question/math-academy-id "e-756"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b839f19a-161c-5cb4-bc08-990f09afb900" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050734}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530545}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maclaurin Expansion of a Logarithmic Function"} :step/id #uuid "71fa7742-aee9-5fb7-8556-20e4c6b386ba" :step/math-academy-id 2576 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088672}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088672 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087790 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087636 :question/id #uuid "f4e495eb-3712-5106-abce-ab07f15fb328" :question/math-academy-id "e-757"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de71fcb6-36ca-581a-92ea-47c1d0c6bf10" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049500}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536813}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maclaurin Expansion of a Product of Functions Using Standard Maclaurin Expansions"} :step/id #uuid "24d27f91-863a-5781-8829-9273038dd369" :step/math-academy-id 2578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088693 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088968 :tutorial/content "In order to quickly compute the Maclaurin expansions of a variety of functions, it's worth memorizing some of the most basic Maclaurin expansions so that you can use them as starting points. These are summarized below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {e}^{x}=1+x+\\frac{1}{2!}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3!}{x}^{3}+⋯\\, &  & x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ) \\\\  & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)=x-\\frac{1}{3!}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{5!}{x}^{5}-⋯\\, &  & x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ) \\\\  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)=1-\\frac{1}{2!}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4!}{x}^{4}-⋯\\, &  & x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ) \\\\  & \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)=x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-⋯\\, &  & x\\in (-1,1] \\\\  & \\frac{1}{1-x}=1+x+{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}+⋯\\, &  & x\\in (-1,1) \\\\  & \\frac{1}{1+x}=1-x+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}+⋯ &  & x\\in (-1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** To memorize the Maclaurin expansions above, it's helpful to notice the following:\n\n- The series for $\\dfrac{1}{1-x}$ consists a geometric series whose first term is $1$ and whose common ratio is $x.$ This makes sense, because the sum formula for a geometric series with first term $a_1$ and common ratio $r$ is $\\dfrac{a_1}{1-r}.$\n\n- The series for $\\sin (x)$ consists of the alternating sum of the odd-degree terms in the series for $e^x.$ This makes sense because $\\sin(x)$ is an odd function (its graph is symmetric across the origin).\n\n- The series for $\\cos (x)$ consists of the alternating sum of the even-degree terms in the series for $e^x.$ This makes sense because $\\cos(x)$ is an even function (its graph is symmetric across the $y$-axis).\n\n- The series for $e^x,$ $\\sin(x),$ and $\\cos(x)$ all have a factorial in the denominator and no factorial in the numerator. For all of our standard series, any term with a factorial in the denominator will converge for all $x,$ because the factorial grows much more quickly than the powers of $x.$ So, their intervals of convergence are $x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1801e6eb-5326-5b15-a38c-b91f970d7a15" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4612 :tutorial/title "The Basic Maclaurin Expansions"} :step/id #uuid "5dc233c4-4b38-5913-8b2d-1f2421ba65d7" :step/math-academy-id 28401 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088669}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088694 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088969 :tutorial/content "To find the Maclaurin expansion for a sum, difference, or product of functions, we can\n\n1\\. find the Maclaurin expansions of the individual functions, and then\n\n2\\. perform the corresponding operation on the expansions.\n\nThe only catch is that we must take the intersection of the intervals of convergence, since we want to find where *both* of the individual functions converge.\n\nFor example, recall the following two Maclaurin expansions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {e}^{x}=1+x+\\frac{1}{2!}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3!}{x}^{3}+⋯\\, &  & x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ) \\\\  & \\frac{1}{1-x}=1+x+{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}+⋯\\, &  & x\\in (-1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the Maclaurin expansion of $e^x+\\dfrac{1}{1-x},$ we can simply add the two expansions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{1-x} & =(1+x+\\frac{1}{2!}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3!}{x}^{3}+⋯)+(1+x+{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}+⋯) \\\\  & =(1+1)+(1+1)x+(\\frac{1}{2!}+1){x}^{2}+(\\frac{1}{3!}+1){x}^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =2+2x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the interval of convergence, we take the intersection of the intervals of convergence of the individual functions:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty, \\infty) \\cap (-1, 1) = (-1, 1)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{1-x}=2+2x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{3}+⋯,\\, &  & x\\in (-1,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "29e51064-92e4-53b6-870b-05fec1aa937f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4613 :tutorial/title "The Maclaurin Expansion of a Sum, Difference, or Product of Functions"} :step/id #uuid "a2fb2ac1-05c2-545a-b8fc-870f2fe95150" :step/math-academy-id 28402 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088670}}] :activity/title "Representing Functions as Power Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087950}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158242} :activity/id #uuid "8990e9d3-9dab-50b9-8dcc-34f9c6fb00b9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049383 :topic/id #uuid "f081dc8e-cc66-5491-a79c-ec9dc25a323b" :topic/math-academy-id 2976} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158242 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158535 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = x^2-y\n$$\n\nWe can deduce a lot of information about the solutions to this equation without necessarily solving it.\n\nFor example, suppose we're interested in the solution curve $y = y(x)$ that passes through the point $P(0,0).$ We can find the slope of the solution curve at this point by substituting the coordinates of $P$ into the right-hand side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,0)} & ={({x}^{2}-y)|}_{(0,0)} \\\\  & ={0}^{2}-0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the derivative is zero at $P,$ we conclude that $P$ is a stationary point. We can confirm that this is the case by using a computer package to plot the particular solution $y=y(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/11/115b16ea6c6d9f52c5a867249f273aaceb43f820a9f7762c3368663950e9eecc.png)\n\nMany differential equations are difficult to integrate. Therefore, it is important to be able to deduce information about the solutions without integrating the equation." :tutorial/id #uuid "845b15c3-96e9-5632-a251-12626046df89" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4766 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "85106edf-2fc2-5e92-a30a-c51ed6662ae7" :step/math-academy-id 29506 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158243}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158243 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157363 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157180 :question/id #uuid "78635415-4c41-5fd4-8758-ed522da90e01" :question/math-academy-id "e-7918"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5586c38-e0ca-5a6d-90ab-71c0fead2cc2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048385}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357427}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Point on a Solution Curve Is a Stationary Point"} :step/id #uuid "3baee2c8-b8dd-51a5-b113-e5e110eb9580" :step/math-academy-id 29507 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158248}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158248 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157367 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157184 :question/id #uuid "2e9d5626-bb05-5382-91f4-efa0235b09be" :question/math-academy-id "e-7923"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f28fbcc-6d9c-5dc7-89cc-13255db78765" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050902}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548038}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Second Derivative at a Point on a Solution Curve"} :step/id #uuid "5a2deab5-5f28-5cc0-b3bf-d4add49c3d27" :step/math-academy-id 29524 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158249}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158249 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157368 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157185 :question/id #uuid "ce6ca955-28f1-5912-bd36-dc6ef7be1241" :question/math-academy-id "e-7924"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049604}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357441}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"} :step/id #uuid "6cd5206c-1e2f-50a8-be4f-738814db7d53" :step/math-academy-id 29527}] :activity/title "Qualitative Analysis of First-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157524}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176790} :activity/id #uuid "16557756-a3b7-5558-ab5e-a8955663d6c3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049441 :topic/id #uuid "f5629308-c63f-5edc-936d-919c173f2a47" :topic/math-academy-id 3668} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176633 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175921 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175779 :question/id #uuid "4bd17973-6b8a-51d6-b4c2-fb941cc1f24d" :question/math-academy-id "e-537"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e367aafb-2474-5dfc-a45f-be3a5ea76942" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548034}] :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Two Steps"} :step/id #uuid "9b7066b5-7785-5214-82a7-4a179f269b09" :step/math-academy-id 1894 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176792}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176634 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177098 :tutorial/content "When applying Euler's method, an important point to keep in mind is that the smaller the step size is, the better our approximation will be. \n\nTo demonstrate, consider the graph below.\n\n![](images/80/80d62e063823096c1ba6896227ff8d03f10786d1117d70002985e78a14a46655.png)\n\nThe solid curve represents the actual solution $y = y(x)$ to an initial value problem with initial condition $y(x_0) = y_0.$ The dotted curves represent some Euler's approximations to the same initial value problem:\n\n- The blue dots represent the approximation obtained using Euler's method with $\\Delta x = 0.05.$\n\n- The red dots represent the approximation obtained using Euler's method with $\\Delta x = 0.25.$\n\nThe diagram shows that the sequence of points with the smaller step size approximates the actual solution better than the sequence with the larger step size." :tutorial/id #uuid "a9ac2e4e-052b-51b2-9f41-ee7bac19f763" :tutorial/math-academy-id 969 :tutorial/title "The Importance of Step Size in Euler's Method"} :step/id #uuid "660295f1-95d1-56e0-b591-fce238e8c6d6" :step/math-academy-id 1897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176687 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175959 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175808 :question/id #uuid "344b786f-0c6c-5ea4-9493-c787df2543ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-7701"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e53619d-67f3-5992-9280-0f884214eca6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048948}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357382}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Second Row of a Table Using Euler's Method"} :step/id #uuid "b7c95bae-98c7-5473-ab7a-fd3e93fc1513" :step/math-academy-id 28638 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176633}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176790 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177026 :tutorial/content "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x+y+1, \\qquad y(0)=2\n$$\n\nOur goal here is to approximate $y(1)$ *and* $y(2)$ using Euler's method. \n\n![](images/88/88b9a68dd2f5982b14a1d16588320a4770612eacdd2f1915a5e659c4a89b9760.png)\n\nWe will proceed using two steps of equal size. The step size $\\Delta x$ is\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{2}}-{\\color{red}{0}}}{2} = 1.\n$$\n\nWe will use a table to keep track of all the numbers. Let's start by adding the initial condition data $y(0) = 2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nWe now proceed in two steps.\n\n**Step 1**: We compute $y'$ using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row of the table, and we calculate $\\Delta y$ using Euler's method.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =x+y+1 \\\\  & =0+2+1 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{'}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =2+3 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFilling in our table, we get the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $1$ | $5$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**: We now repeat the process. We compute $y'$ using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *second* row of the table, and we calculate $\\Delta y$ using Euler's method.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =x+y+1 \\\\  & =1+5+1 \\\\  & =7 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{'}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =7\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find new values for $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =5+7 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFilling in our table, we get the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $1$ | $5$ | $7$ | $7$ |\n| $2$ | $12$ |  |  |\n\nLooking at the two leftmost columns, we conclude that $y(1)\\approx 5$ and $y(2)\\approx 12.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "139fbc6d-df3c-58ad-9e25-073c549a161d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5690 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "93de155f-ab7f-5540-9acb-23b205726c7b" :step/math-academy-id 34943 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176687}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176792 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176017 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175872 :question/id #uuid "29a57695-57ff-566d-8231-bab1bc99a240" :question/math-academy-id "e-9354"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16629348-2577-5c39-8c8c-0f24dbb52c87" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398214}] :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Three Steps"} :step/id #uuid "48e7c710-7513-58d4-b6e5-f52fee9a3d7f" :step/math-academy-id 35018 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176634}}] :activity/title "Euler's Method: Calculating Multiple Steps" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176171}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088678} :activity/id #uuid "b3fd6ef1-04ad-5c0a-b327-86d38cf19448" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049763 :topic/id #uuid "11d9e0d4-4303-597d-9364-282253c19d56" :topic/math-academy-id 886} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088678 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088853 :tutorial/content "Consider the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\cos \\sqrt{x} \\, \\textrm dx\\,\n$$\n\nUnfortunately, we cannot compute this integral using any of our standard methods. However, we *can* express this integral as an infinite series if we first write the integrand $\\cos \\sqrt{x}$ as a Maclaurin series.\n\nLet's start with the Maclaurin series expansion of $\\cos{x},$ valid for all $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\cos{x}\t=1 - \\frac{1}{2!}x^2 + \\frac{1}{4!}x^4+\\cdots \\,.\n$$\n\n    \n\nReplacing $x$ with $\\sqrt{x}$ in the above equation, we find the series expansion for $\\cos{\\sqrt{x}}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x} & =1-\\frac{1}{2!}(\\sqrt{x})^{2}+\\frac{1}{4!}(\\sqrt{x})^{4}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{24}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n        \n\nIntegrating both sides of this equality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x}\\,dx & =\\int (1-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{24}+⋯)\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{24}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+⋯ \\\\  & =C+x-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =C+x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{72}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n        \n\nAnd we're done! We've just found the Maclaurin series expression for $\\displaystyle \\int\\cos{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.$\n\nWe can apply the same method to any function $f(x)$ having a power series expansion of the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=a_0+a_1 x+a_2 x^2 + \\cdots\\,.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({a}_{0}+{a}_{1}x+{a}_{2}{x}^{2}+⋯)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={a}_{0}\\int \\,\\text{d}x+{a}_{1}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+{a}_{2}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+⋯ \\\\  & =C+{a}_{0}x+{a}_{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{a}_{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is valid for the same radius of convergence as the series expansion of $f(x).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b0a3a223-98fa-56e3-b1df-6cb6d1bcdaf1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1322 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6ceb1bdb-03e5-5b90-9089-ebb348602688" :step/math-academy-id 2600 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088679}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088679 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087793 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087639 :question/id #uuid "16b3f250-dfcb-5fa6-bfdb-4acf92d8bd37" :question/math-academy-id "e-765"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eea68c90-99a8-5254-b799-5f0ed51615a9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050321}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532725}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Indefinite Integrals Using Taylor Series"} :step/id #uuid "3701ede4-42d5-5b06-ae18-a15fcdbae21e" :step/math-academy-id 2601 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088680}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088680 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087794 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087640 :question/id #uuid "4211b707-bff1-5855-9449-16e14858e6ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-766"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f133583-cb11-5007-bdef-641838aff5e4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050819}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548037}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Definite Integrals Using Taylor Series"} :step/id #uuid "f3e25ee3-5f82-5928-a669-03387e398d24" :step/math-academy-id 2604}] :activity/title "Approximating Integrals Using Taylor Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087951}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055648} :activity/id #uuid "a47ac5b8-841f-560b-8cb9-a5aa92267125" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050041 :topic/id #uuid "28124701-15c1-562d-b441-8c061aed5e28" :topic/math-academy-id 36} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055648 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055932 :tutorial/content "Suppose the Taylor (Maclaurin) series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{2n+1} x^{2n+1}\n$$\n\nand converges to $f(x)$ for $|x| < R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. How can we calculate the first three non-zero terms of Taylor series for the derivative, $f'(x),$ about $x=0?$\n\nAll we need to do is differentiate the Taylor series of $f(x).$ Writing out the first three terms, we get\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{2n+1} x^{2n+1} = x - \\dfrac{x^3}{3} + \\dfrac{x^5}{5} - \\cdots.\n$$\n\nNow, to find $f'(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5})-⋯ \\\\  & =1-{x}^{2}+{x}^{4}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! The radius of convergence of the Taylor series for $f'(x)$ is $R,$ the same as $f(x).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "59207cd1-3d1f-52ac-9acb-354bc8dff029" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1318 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0d27720d-4ea4-522a-9aab-b1b543167de5" :step/math-academy-id 2591 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055649}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055649 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054682 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054581 :question/id #uuid "3c7dd3ad-eaa9-5312-adaf-3a0772e845f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-762"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67e6a951-2645-5440-8059-14f0182933ff" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547987}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Few Terms for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"} :step/id #uuid "24137bfa-62c0-5f75-81c4-d5f0a97c7f3b" :step/math-academy-id 2592 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055650}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055650 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054683 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054582 :question/id #uuid "cb7a106a-130a-5479-b60d-8ea76a8cd8de" :question/math-academy-id "e-763"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d418552d-d147-5736-8acd-5ba237a90e07" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528007}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Term for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"} :step/id #uuid "a87c1316-a593-50aa-a57c-45932bf5e5df" :step/math-academy-id 2595 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055651}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055651 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054684 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054583 :question/id #uuid "e656ee8d-55b9-5ea6-beae-7c2caf329a6a" :question/math-academy-id "e-764"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88b9751d-450e-5ae9-bda6-1c1d136bc929" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050624}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262596} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262597} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528012}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Convergence for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"} :step/id #uuid "db8d259a-9396-5153-933f-88b2dad6b30c" :step/math-academy-id 2598}] :activity/title "Differentiating Taylor Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054898}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093128} :activity/id #uuid "2bfd4e6c-ec75-50d4-afdf-91c3c5286aba" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050624 :topic/id #uuid "59d3ddba-0cbf-5ffd-9492-0f3a847c20ec" :topic/math-academy-id 984} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093128 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093372 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we are given the **infinite power series**\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{x^n}{n^2}=x +\\frac{x^2}{2^2}+\\frac{x^3}{3^2}+\\frac{x^4}{4^2} +\\cdots \\,.\n$$\n\nThis series is a function of $x,$ and it may converge for some values of $x$ and diverge for other values. How can we determine the values of $x$ for which the series is convergent?\n\nOne method that often works is the ratio test for convergence. We define $a_n=\\dfrac{x^n}{n^2},$ and we calculate the limit of the ratio of two successive terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{2}}{{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{2}}{(n+1)^{2}}\\,|x| \\\\  & =|x|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{2}}{(n+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =|x|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}+2n+1} \\\\  & =|x|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{1+\\frac{2}{n}+\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}} \\\\  & =|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe ratio test says that the series converges if $L<1,$ diverges if $L>1,$ and is inconclusive if $L=1.$ So, the series converges if $|x|<1.$ Therefore, the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-1,1).$\n\nUnfortunately, the ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence when $|x|=1,$ so the endpoints $x=1$ and $x=-1$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{x^n}{n^2}= \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{n^2} = 1 +\\frac{1}{2^2}+\\frac{1}{3^2}+\\frac{1}{4^2} +\\cdots \\,,\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the $p$-series test.\n\n- When $x=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{x^n}{n^2}= \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{(-1)^n}{n^2} = -1 +\\frac{1}{2^2}-\\frac{1}{3^2}+\\frac{1}{4^2} +\\cdots\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent provided that $x \\in [-1,1].$ We call the interval $[-1,1]$ the **interval of convergence**.\n\nThe **radius of convergence** $R$ is half of the length of the interval (in the same way that the radius of a circle is equal to half the diameter of the circle). In our case, we have\n\n$$\nR = \\dfrac{1-(-1)}{2} = 1.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f42b2bfc-490f-56a9-876e-52951e6626f8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1296 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "fa1b44db-cb7e-5e5a-825c-317e510f7037" :step/math-academy-id 2549 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093129}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093129 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092269 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092130 :question/id #uuid "518c7492-3269-506d-9ae6-7a9d26f9f748" :question/math-academy-id "e-747"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72ee4476-9ccd-5e3a-a1aa-efe576b4d55e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050182}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548024}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Convergence of a Given Power Series"} :step/id #uuid "fed0ee6e-6bc0-5a6e-88b9-7c10c0598b04" :step/math-academy-id 2550 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093137}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093130 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092270 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092131 :question/id #uuid "d50d01de-9116-53bf-9414-a148dbb64ad1" :question/math-academy-id "e-748"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f57a9fc-0051-54e3-9c07-42ef3f9e266e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259596} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259597} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351460} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362345}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius and Interval of Convergence of a Power Series when the Limit of the Ratio is Zero"} :step/id #uuid "85321812-f63c-5e55-af3d-47e8a8ee0a02" :step/math-academy-id 2554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093137 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092276 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092117 :question/id #uuid "fdd23348-35c5-5a1e-9651-3a41a7df40f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7274"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "beb5fa66-9dc2-50aa-beaa-310da7fc4c6b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050498}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405002}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Interval of Convergence of a Given Power Series"} :step/id #uuid "8f43b43a-e79a-5bf4-883f-76a9c8294a38" :step/math-academy-id 26935 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093130}}] :activity/title "Radius of Convergence of Power Series Centered at the Origin" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092459}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101715} :activity/id #uuid "a9b4379c-c4ae-5316-aee1-bbb38b252dff" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050905 :topic/id #uuid "701ed6eb-4bd0-5344-bf15-d9a52b59f3af" :topic/math-academy-id 1179} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101715 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102032 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find a solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\ny' = (1-2x)y^2 \\,,\n$$\n\nsatisfying the additional condition $y(0)=1.$ This additional condition $y(0)=1$ is called an **initial condition** or sometimes a **boundary condition** of the differential equation. \n\nFirst of all, we note that the given differential equation is separable, so we can separate variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(1-2x){y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1-2x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{{y}^{2}}\\, & =\\int (1-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =x-{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & ={x}^{2}-x-C \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-C}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\ny    =   \\dfrac {1} {x^2 - x - C} \\,,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nNow we choose the constant $C$ such that the condition $y(0)=1$ is satisfied. Substituting $x=0$ and $y=1$ into the general solution and solving for $C,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0)=\\frac{1}{0-0-C}=1\\,\\Rightarrow \\,C=-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution of $y' = (1-2x)y^2$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny    =   \\dfrac {1} {x^2 - x - (-1)} = \\dfrac {1} {x^2 - x + 1}\\,.\n$$\n\nTo recap: the solution of a first-order separable ordinary differential equation (ODE) with an initial condition is obtained by \n\n- separating the variables,\n\n- integrating the equation, and then\n\n- substituting the condition into the solution to solve for the constant of integration." :tutorial/id #uuid "e2c4c33d-a1bd-537e-9daa-1960f765953d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 974 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2b9a5578-4e53-599e-9d11-d900a008d201" :step/math-academy-id 1907 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101716}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101716 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100893 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100728 :question/id #uuid "dbb4e0a0-55ec-5cc7-9617-99cec8108940" :question/math-academy-id "e-540"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99112850-043d-53e5-8d61-fecb0317bd11" :knowledge-point/key-